==================================== INPUT ====================================
// Test: `@UNSTABLE_key` grants exactly the fields it names, and nothing else.
//
// Identical to `exec_time_s2c_root_fragment_allows_key_field` — same query, same
// `extend type User @UNSTABLE_key(fields: "actor_key")` — except the root fragment reads
// `name`, which the annotation does not cover. Nothing promises `name` arrives in
// the payload, so it stays a compile error.
//
// Without this control the positive fixture would pass just as well against a
// runtime that treated `@UNSTABLE_key`'s mere PRESENCE on the type as blanket permission.
//- FooQuery.js
graphql`query FooQuery @exec_time_resolvers {
  me {
    greeting
  }
}`

//- User_greeting.js
/**
 * @relayField User.greeting: RelayResolverValue
 * @rootFragment UserGreetingFragment
 */
graphql`fragment UserGreetingFragment on User {
  id
  name
}`

//- relay.config.json
{
  "language": "flow",
  "jsModuleFormat": "haste",
  "schema": "schema.graphql",
  "schemaExtensions": ["schema-extensions"],
  "featureFlags": {
    "enable_exec_time_resolvers_directive": true
  }
}

//- schema.graphql
type Query {
  me: User
}

type User {
  id: ID!
  name: String
  actor_key: ID!
}

//- schema-extensions/extension.graphql
extend type User @UNSTABLE_key(fields: "actor_key")
==================================== OUTPUT ===================================
✖︎ Server-to-client resolver @rootFragment `UserGreetingFragment` in exec time resolvers may only select `__typename` and/or `id`. Found disallowed selection: name. S2C resolvers must use identity-only @rootFragment.

  User_greeting.js:7:3
    6 │   id
    7 │   name
      │   ^^^^
    8 │ }
